## Lore

Minotaurs are an old race of bull-headed giants, famed as fighters for their strength, stamina and speed. Cautious counts put fewer than 8,000 of them left in the world, on the brink of dying out; other scholars answer that nobody has any real idea, and that reports from recent centuries could support a number twenty times as high.

They live underground in the most remote and hostile country there is: the Old Kingdom Mountains, the Great Northern Mountains, the Baalgor Wastelands, and by rumour the Land of the Damned, where a Minotaur city is said to stand. They are met singly, in pairs or in small bands, and tribes are small. Secretive and violent, they attack whoever strays into their tunnels, lost traveller or not, and bear a particular grudge against humans, elves and dwarves. So few outsiders have met one and lived that most people think the race a legend.

Even a friendly Minotaur knows little of his people''s past beyond this: some catastrophe struck them thousands of years ago and they have hidden from the world since. Many tie it to their willing service to the Old Ones, or to an attempt to wake them. Death cults devoted to the Old Ones and other dark gods have surfaced among them again and again, and old tales speak of Minotaur necromancers, witches and sorcerers. Elven records describe a ruined Minotaur city under the old Baalgor Rainforest, its statues beheaded and its carvings defaced, and mass graves holding tens of thousands. Minotaur graveyards still dot the Wastelands.

A few Minotaurs, good and evil alike, leave the tunnels for a life of adventure on the surface, mostly in the Eastern Territory, the Old Kingdom, the Baalgor Wastelands and the Yin-Sloth Jungles.

**Alignment:** Any, but most are evil or selfish.

**O.C.C.s available:** Any. Nearly all are men at arms, mercenaries and assassins especially; knights and palladins are extremely rare, and a few become clergy or mages.

**Physical Appearance:** Muscular humanoid giants with the head of a bull, black hair, yellow eyes, and skin from light or dark bluish-gray to black.

**Size:** 8 to 11 feet tall (2.4 to 3.3 m); 7 feet plus 1D4 additional feet. **Weight:** 500 to 800 pounds (225 to 360 kg).

**Average Life Span:** 400 years; few live past 500.

**Languages:** Troll/Giantese, Gobblely and Elven. Most are illiterate.

**Enemies:** No humanoid is trusted at first. A few Minotaurs do form lasting bonds with outsiders, but only with someone who has shown over time that he is reliable and accepts them.

**Allies:** The race has none.

**Habitat:** The southern half of the known world, chiefly the Old Kingdom Mountains, the Baalgor Wastelands and parts of the Yin-Sloth Jungles.

## GM Notes

Encounters: alone, in pairs, or in bands of 2D4+1. A tribe numbers about 2D6x10; the largest are thought to exceed 300 and are rare. A typical Minotaur knows only the 1D4 small tribes nearest his own.

Of every hundred Minotaurs, 94 are men at arms: 40% of those are mercenaries, 20% assassins, and the rest spread over the other fighting classes. A Minotaur knight or palladin, above all one who champions the light, is branded a traitor and cast out; such a pariah can expect fear and mistrust from other races and from Minotaurs too. The other six in a hundred are clergy or mages and tend to lead or advise their communities: about 2% Chaos Priests (priests of darkness serving the Old Ones), 1.5% Disciples of the Old Ones (witches pacted to them), 1.5% Necromancers, and 1% wizards, earth warlocks and other spell casters.

Baalgor Minotaurs can dig, but would sooner move into abandoned Dwarven works, Baalizad burrows, caves, dungeons and catacombs, and many are skilled at rigging their tunnels with traps and pits. Most are half nomadic. Half are vegetarian, and even the meat eaters prefer grass, grain, vegetables and fruit; they forage on the surface and hurry back below. They love heavy armor and weapons but are fair to poor crafters, so most of what they carry was taken from the dead.

Value: a horn can fetch as much as 1,500 gold from an alchemist. Buyers inspect them closely, since a bull''s horn is easily passed off as one.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'minotaur');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'minotaur';

